00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:37.710
First

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.960
is Ma Thida,

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.553
Who is a surgeon and novelist.

00:00:47.639 --> 00:00:52.533
A large figure in the
international PEN movement.

00:00:53.827 --> 00:00:54.660
And...

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:58.550
Very well known as an activist.

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:02.343
And so let me turn over the
floor to her for seven minutes.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:05.040
- Thank you, everybody, and

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.290
I think it's very hard to cover

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.100
what's happening right now in my country

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.830
in seven minutes, but I'll try my best.

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.760
And first of all, I would like to focus on

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:17.764
the governance issue.

00:01:17.764 --> 00:01:21.920
After the coup, the
military, it cannot show

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:26.060
it's qualified enough
to govern the country,

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:30.200
especially in Magway
division, Sagaing division

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.550
and Chin states, of course.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:36.330
And then the Chin state and the Yangon,

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:38.580
it's not as the whole region,

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.883
but especially in the city area.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:45.770
At the same time, they
are trying to control,

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:49.870
through the village level
administrators on the ground

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:51.850
for the majority of the country.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:55.460
In that case, from the revolution side,

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:57.920
people defense forces are trying to focus

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.070
on these local administrator,

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:06.070
as they are precursors to send
all the information to the...

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:12.750
Soldiers or the military; Whom to arrest,

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.560
whom to kill, et cetera.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:18.490
That's why the current situation

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:20.740
about the governance issue is pretty much

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.720
in a question mark.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:27.627
And PDF focuses on
killing this kind of the,

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:31.500
local village level, administrators,

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.330
naming them as Dalan, the informer.

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:37.283
So in terms of the
legislature, the military,

00:02:38.244 --> 00:02:40.670
since the coup, they don't care about...

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:46.880
Either initiation or the
amendment of any law,

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:49.344
apart from the cybersecurity law.

00:02:49.344 --> 00:02:51.420
In that case, right now,

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.170
the People Defense Forces
and the revolution people

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:58.803
focusing on destroying the Mytel tower.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:01.470
From the very beginning,

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:05.400
since the military do the
internet shut down deliberately

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.020
in the whole country.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:11.510
But right now, focusing on
this hard to govern area,

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.823
they do the international (indistinct).

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:17.273
And right now in Sagaing,

00:03:17.273 --> 00:03:20.230
and in part of the Chin state.

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:25.230
But at the same times the PDF
focuses on the Mytel tower,

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:28.340
and then the Mytel owned by the military,

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:31.710
partly owned by Vietnamese
business company.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:35.570
Then the Mytel started saying
about internet shut down

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:37.370
will marginally interfere

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:40.660
for the sake of the
information, et cetera.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:45.010
Even though the military
introduced the cybersecurity law,

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:47.090
and all the...

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:52.090
The white list introduction and etcetera,

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:53.640
as soon as they took the power.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:58.510
But NUG has been doing
quite a lot of the...

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:01.750
Law enforce, not,

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:04.340
law amendment or the initiation.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:09.117
Recently the NUG started
the income tax law,

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:12.440
that the amendment of the income tax law

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:17.440
in order to have more
donation, indirect donations,

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:20.030
and from the business people.

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:22.560
Rich business people and Italy...

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:28.020
Citizens, they normally don't
take part in the revolutions

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:32.000
through the protest, nor through the

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.610
any other ways.

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:35.470
But they have the money,

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:40.190
they can indirectly
support the revolution.

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:42.330
This is also very, pretty much,

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:45.033
the ground situation on the legislature.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:49.690
In terms of rule of law...

00:04:51.198 --> 00:04:54.240
The killing, and arbitrary arrests,

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.520
looting, asking for the ransoms for the...

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.200
The arrested of people,
or the dead bodies,

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.530
the arsons they're
burning the whole village,

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:05.600
and bombing.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.100
This is going on from the military side,

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.370
and they sometimes accuse
the People Defense Forces

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:14.950
as the corporate for that.

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:18.480
Even though they already
have their own version

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:20.500
of the civilian...

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:24.640
Violence attackers, under
the name of (indistinct),

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:25.960
something like that.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:29.470
But the way they look
at the rule of law issue

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:31.410
is for example recently...

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:39.100
The daughter of the hiring
military officer has been shooted

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:43.070
at very late night when she was
driving back from somewhere.

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:47.220
And the security forces stopped
her and she didn't stop,

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:50.500
and she kept driving and
she was shooted to death.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:52.920
And in that case, the military supporters

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:55.293
started saying that, "Oh, you see,

00:05:55.293 --> 00:05:57.610
our military is good enough.

00:05:57.610 --> 00:06:01.300
They don't care whoever betrayed the law,

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:03.430
they are taking action.

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:06.651
They never ever think
this should not be done,

00:06:06.651 --> 00:06:11.651
the killing to those people
without knowing them...

00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:14.560
It's arbitrary killing.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:18.650
But the way of the concept
of the military supporter

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:21.460
on rule of law is something like that.

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:24.800
But at the same times we have so much

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:28.210
concern about the...

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:31.080
For longer term problems.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:34.490
If everybody just thinking

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:37.360
of the UMP revolution is the solution.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:40.720
But recently I was so
happy to learn the CDM,

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:43.550
the Civil Disobedience Police.

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:46.810
They started making an announcement,

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:49.950
saying that they beg the
pardons from the people

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:53.640
for the institutional...

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:59.900
Wrongdoing, during the
Saffron Revolution in 2007,

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:02.150
even though they are not the real culprit,

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:06.390
or real committer of this crime.

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:09.130
They feel the institution
has a responsibility,

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:11.863
and they tried to beg the
pardon from the people.

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:17.910
The way the revolutions grew...

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:20.350
Thinking on the issue of rule of law,

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:23.200
is quite promising to me in that case.

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:27.320
And yes, the last thing is
all about the resilience

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:29.010
and the resistance.

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:30.410
It's not just from the people,

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:31.913
and we learning more and more,

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:35.820
the soldiers are defecting.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:39.090
And the way the...

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:41.440
Revolution people,

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:43.380
resistance is...

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:46.900
More like...

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:50.040
Just trying to stop
the military data ship,

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:53.640
not focusing on the...

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:55.680
Violence for the longer run.

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:57.340
So that we'll cover.

00:07:57.340 --> 00:07:59.200
Thank you, thank you.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:00.480
- Thank you Ma Thida.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:04.200
The next speaker (clears
throat) is Katun Myint,

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:08.430
a professor in political
science at Carlton College.

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:12.870
He was a student leader
in the 1988 uprising...

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:17.545
As a PhD from Indiana.

00:08:17.545 --> 00:08:19.940
Wrote a book on international treaties

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:22.640
concerning the (indistinct) and the Rhine.

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:27.120
And he is a founder of
the Mutual Aid Myanmar,

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:32.120
that has gathered lots of
contributions to help...

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:34.873
Striking public sector workers.

00:08:35.910 --> 00:08:37.570
Katun, please.

00:08:37.570 --> 00:08:38.890
- Thank you very much Jim,

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:43.860
and thank you to all of you who
are listening to this panel.

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:48.860
And I will offer two broad
historical related frameworks,

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:52.390
two broad frameworks
related to, or rooted,

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:53.840
in Burmese history.

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:57.843
And then one philosophical
reflection on the current crisis.

00:08:58.903 --> 00:08:59.780
I'll start with the first one,

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:03.690
the first one is related to
China and Burma relation.

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:08.690
And that has to go back to
the end of Sino Burmese war...

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:11.383
At the end of 1769.

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:19.030
One emperor of Qin dynasty, Qin Long,

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:22.890
decided to end the forth war,

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:25.260
attempting to annex Burma.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:29.460
He issued a proclamation
to his military leaders,

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:32.540
in that military leaders program mission.

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:34.650
This is what a Qin Long said,

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:36.827
and quote, beginning of the quote,

00:09:36.827 --> 00:09:40.390
"When you sent the army and in tech,

00:09:40.390 --> 00:09:44.090
there should be a fewer
soldiers lightly armed.

00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:49.090
They should take advantage of
the enemies lack of readiness

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:52.450
to just destroy their crops.

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:56.370
And kill a few and take a few prisoners.

00:09:56.370 --> 00:10:00.160
The purpose is no more
than to disturb them,

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:03.830
but not really a war of big scale."

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:07.990
So that heading, which the Qin Long issue,

00:10:07.990 --> 00:10:11.400
still in play today in terms of creating

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:13.830
buffer zones on the Thai-Burma.

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:16.030
Sorry, Myanmar and China border.

00:10:16.030 --> 00:10:18.904
Wa ethnic groups, Chin and (indistinct),

00:10:18.904 --> 00:10:22.030
and AA, which was born in KIA area.

00:10:22.910 --> 00:10:27.120
All play in that game of the end of a war.

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:30.930
But what Qin Long wanted was
a permanent war in Burma,

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:33.190
where have no peace can be settled.

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:36.520
And so today's ethnic conflict.

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:40.270
And then the march to the
federal open inclusive framework

00:10:40.270 --> 00:10:43.715
is a struggle here China
has posed on Burma.

00:10:43.715 --> 00:10:46.590
Wa, (indistinct), and
all other ethnic group

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:49.460
that has not been explicitly saying

00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:50.460
that we support NUG.

00:10:51.318 --> 00:10:54.520
NUG has been supported
by people and so on.

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:57.520
That framework is pretty important here,

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:01.330
because endless war is what China wanted,

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:04.480
that is happening in Myanmar,
and it is happening now.

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:08.863
So second thing that he did
was on February 6th, 1770,

00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:14.640
he issue economic (indistinct)
against the Burmese king.

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:20.050
This might be perhaps the
first economic sanction

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:22.600
imposed by a country
against another country

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:24.250
in the world history.

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:29.250
It is certainly the first
economic sanction issue in Asia,

00:11:29.270 --> 00:11:30.720
Asian continent.

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:34.550
In that economic session, his ethic set,

00:11:34.550 --> 00:11:36.000
I'm going to quote him again,

00:11:36.877 --> 00:11:38.640
"As for the matter of trait,

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:42.380
the Myanmar bandits eager
to exchange with China.

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:45.640
This is shown clearly in
several letters they sent.

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:47.790
Fortunately, I have foreseen this,

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:51.960
and I ordered repeatedly that
they could only ask for it

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:54.300
after they submit to us.

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:57.270
In short, no trade with
China will be allowed

00:11:57.270 --> 00:12:00.350
before the arrival of
their surrender letter.

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:01.840
This method is the best way

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:04.500
to control the foreign barbarians."

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:08.927
So that was his ethic on this
issue on February 6th, 1770,

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:11.360
against Burma.

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:16.070
And so that trait practice
is still useful today,

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:19.530
to understand the Chinese
investment in Burma,

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:22.710
the economic influence
that China is exercising

00:12:22.710 --> 00:12:24.610
and maneuvering this crisis in Myanmar.

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:28.270
In fact, to support the
first goal that they had,

00:12:28.270 --> 00:12:30.970
which is just basically,
Burma should never get rich,

00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:32.130
never have peace.

00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:36.220
And that is Chinese foreign
policy rooted in 1770,

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:38.270
and it is continuing today.

00:12:38.270 --> 00:12:41.260
And so those two frameworks
are very important for us

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:44.030
to understand why the Coup, why Mueang

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:47.600
and military leaders are
really totally relying on China

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:51.410
at the UNSC and all of area
of international affairs.

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:52.710
So that's the first one.

00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:54.260
Second one I want to talk about

00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:57.350
is the history of state building in Burma.

00:12:57.350 --> 00:13:00.360
The history of state building
in Burma need to be understood

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:03.900
to understand why the
struggle for federalism,

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:06.823
inclusive open federalism,
is so difficult.

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:08.653
The first...

00:13:09.991 --> 00:13:13.522
The unifier of Burma is
as we know Anawrahta,

00:13:13.522 --> 00:13:15.182
and then the Bayinnaung.

00:13:15.182 --> 00:13:16.950
And then the Alaungpaya.

00:13:18.370 --> 00:13:21.920
And these three King's statues
are erected in Naypyidaw,

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:24.420
and now on the China-Burma border city

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:26.970
is beginning to be built to have that

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:28.860
three statue of these Kings.

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:32.500
What they did and what they
created as a unifying Burma

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:35.970
was a Burman ethnocentric state.

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:38.160
Legitimized by Buddhist stock train,

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:40.900
which supported by the sangha,

00:13:40.900 --> 00:13:43.460
which is a more of a materialist sangha

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:45.760
than the spiritual and philosophical.

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:48.610
And more actually what
Buddha really taught

00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:50.490
to understand the world.

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:52.676
That materialist Buddhism,

00:13:52.676 --> 00:13:56.600
or materialist Buddhists,
good examples are,

00:13:56.600 --> 00:14:01.500
current (indistinct) The
Nationalist Buddhist monks.

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:03.620
All of them are play in
legitimizing the state,

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:06.810
the (indistinct), the full group.

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:11.640
Which is because the
three Burman empire...

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:14.060
Have failed to build the Burman state.

00:14:14.060 --> 00:14:16.020
Now (indistinct) and military leaders

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:19.630
are attempting to build that
state with 2008 constitution.

00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:23.780
And that was their document to
begin unifying Burman state,

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:26.680
and that state failed and by design

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:29.020
because the CDM participant protesters,

00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:32.780
EAO's do not want that
kind of state survive

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:34.410
or thrive in Burma.

00:14:34.410 --> 00:14:35.860
They want that state to fail.

00:14:35.860 --> 00:14:38.560
It's a state failure in
Burma, is it by design?

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:42.950
Society is resilient and
robust against that state.

00:14:42.950 --> 00:14:45.140
And that is really good news for open

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:48.210
and inclusive federalism into the future.

00:14:48.210 --> 00:14:50.490
We can get that into detail in a point.

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:53.290
And the final point that
I wanted to offer is

00:14:54.640 --> 00:14:56.567
my reflection on the current violence.

00:14:56.567 --> 00:14:57.760
The violence that Ma Thida

00:14:57.760 --> 00:15:01.530
have already spoken about
generally, what were happening.

00:15:01.530 --> 00:15:04.308
You can imagine seven years old daughters

00:15:04.308 --> 00:15:08.280
being shot at on the lap
of the father in Mandalay.

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:12.770
You can imagine a poet
killed and open his chest,

00:15:12.770 --> 00:15:15.260
and taking out organs from him.

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:17.933
That will be the poet Khet Thi from Naga.

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:21.070
And the crimes against humanity,

00:15:21.070 --> 00:15:24.350
and the scale of violence, if you will.

00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:27.210
Dirty creativity of the
violence by the Myanmar military

00:15:27.210 --> 00:15:28.183
is stunning.

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:32.510
The world is experiencing and
seeing it every day happening

00:15:32.510 --> 00:15:34.410
unpacked in Burma.

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:36.920
The question for political
scientists, especially,

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:39.090
and maybe even international
lawyers especially, is

00:15:39.090 --> 00:15:43.910
is there a proper violence,
and is there a proper role

00:15:43.910 --> 00:15:47.250
of violence in political establishment?

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:50.860
And I think for us international
relations scholars,

00:15:50.860 --> 00:15:53.430
just war theory, rooted
in Hindu tradition.

00:15:53.430 --> 00:15:56.870
Philosophy offer some
kind of route to actually

00:15:56.870 --> 00:15:59.900
imagine that there might
be proper violence,

00:15:59.900 --> 00:16:02.330
and there might be legitimate violence.

00:16:02.330 --> 00:16:03.940
And there might be just war

00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:08.665
that we could consider, especially if...

00:16:08.665 --> 00:16:12.640
The world is moving
toward a more inclusive,

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:15.530
open liberal framework
of democratic world.

00:16:15.530 --> 00:16:19.480
Against Chinese and Russian
order of centralized order.

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:22.960
If we want to really
open up to the protest,

00:16:22.960 --> 00:16:27.520
or somewhat imagine the agency
and freedom of individuals

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:28.353
around the world.

00:16:28.353 --> 00:16:30.850
In the world order that
is coming into the future,

00:16:30.850 --> 00:16:33.540
we might have to rethink
about the way in which

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:35.330
the utopian peace is imagined.

00:16:35.330 --> 00:16:36.923
So that's all I have to offer.

00:16:39.010 --> 00:16:39.883
- Thanks Katun.

00:16:40.780 --> 00:16:44.293
The next speaker is David Thang Mo.

00:16:45.130 --> 00:16:49.630
He's a PhD candidate in
history and theological studies

00:16:49.630 --> 00:16:54.120
at the Ashbury Theological
Seminar in Kentucky.

00:16:54.120 --> 00:16:55.990
And by going to his website this morning,

00:16:55.990 --> 00:17:00.860
I realized that he's the
author of dozens of articles

00:17:00.860 --> 00:17:05.500
on ethnic conflict, on religion.

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:08.203
On Buddhist nationalism, and so on.

00:17:09.320 --> 00:17:10.950
Some of which I put under my belt,

00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:14.640
but it's quite an impressive reading list.

00:17:14.640 --> 00:17:16.640
And I...

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:18.920
Admire what he's had to say

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:22.090
in several panels that we've
jointly participated in.

00:17:22.090 --> 00:17:23.590
David, you have seven minutes.

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:27.190
- Thank you so much for
your kind introduction

00:17:27.190 --> 00:17:28.100
Professor Jim Scot.

00:17:28.100 --> 00:17:30.540
And it's very good to see you again after

00:17:31.570 --> 00:17:34.083
sharing a panel
(indistinct) in South Korea,

00:17:36.150 --> 00:17:39.510
I will be speaking from the
perspective of the ethnic

00:17:39.510 --> 00:17:41.380
and religious minority.

00:17:41.380 --> 00:17:42.763
So speaking from that,

00:17:43.889 --> 00:17:47.547
it's not possible to ignore
Buddhist nationalism,

00:17:47.547 --> 00:17:49.960
and all the ethnic identity,

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:52.070
or maybe even ideology conflict.

00:17:52.070 --> 00:17:55.433
I just want to focus on that topic today.

00:18:06.270 --> 00:18:08.100
I would like to briefly touch on

00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:11.910
the politics of religions, and the origins

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:14.553
and movement of Buddhist
Nationalism our country.

00:18:15.650 --> 00:18:18.610
Buddhist Nationalism is
nothing new in Myanmar,

00:18:18.610 --> 00:18:21.370
it has been rooted in the
British colonial period,

00:18:21.370 --> 00:18:23.083
in the 19th century.

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:27.410
But the way that Buddhist
Nationalism emerged

00:18:27.410 --> 00:18:30.663
is different from each
department, or each movement.

00:18:31.510 --> 00:18:32.760
In the British colonial period,

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:36.390
it emerged as the anti-Western influence.

00:18:36.390 --> 00:18:39.540
I would like to prefer the
word anti-Western influence,

00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:42.070
instead of anti-foreign influence.

00:18:42.070 --> 00:18:44.940
If we put it anti-foreign
influence we can say that,

00:18:44.940 --> 00:18:48.320
well, now the military
garment is related to China,

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:51.040
and Russia, and all this things like that.

00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:53.350
I prefer the word Buddhist Nationalism,

00:18:53.350 --> 00:18:55.090
emerged in the British colonial period

00:18:55.090 --> 00:18:57.850
as the anti-Western influence.

00:18:57.850 --> 00:19:01.980
It was during that time that
the Burma Independence Army,

00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:04.760
which is the current
military garment, obviously,

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:06.460
that is the rule of the movement.

00:19:06.460 --> 00:19:09.500
It was formed to fight against
the British colonial period,

00:19:09.500 --> 00:19:11.250
and they always claimed that that is how

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:15.370
they came to gain
independence from British.

00:19:15.370 --> 00:19:17.380
Let's move to the second movement,

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:20.141
which is (indistinct)
from the first movement.

00:19:20.141 --> 00:19:22.240
In the post-colonial period,

00:19:22.240 --> 00:19:25.060
or what I'll call it
post-independent period,

00:19:25.060 --> 00:19:28.040
the Buddhist Nationalism
in Myanmar emerged

00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:30.310
as the anti-Burma, or maybe,

00:19:30.310 --> 00:19:33.503
the anti-non-Buddhist movement, right?

00:19:34.370 --> 00:19:38.690
For example, U Nu make a famous slogan,

00:19:38.690 --> 00:19:39.712
what do you call it?

00:19:39.712 --> 00:19:40.880
Amyo, barthar, thathanar.

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:43.870
If you are not a Buddhist in Myanmar,

00:19:43.870 --> 00:19:46.905
you will face some discrimination's.

00:19:46.905 --> 00:19:51.190
There are at least three
main characteristics

00:19:51.190 --> 00:19:52.620
of that Buddhist Nationalism.

00:19:52.620 --> 00:19:55.620
First political domination
is very obvious,

00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:59.300
and the second is that the
ethnic cultural lsf;sk relations.

00:19:59.300 --> 00:20:02.080
So the ethnic minority cultures
has been as it's related

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:05.500
to the dominant Buddhist cultures.

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:08.300
And then the third is the
religious discrimination,

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:09.870
so if you are not a Buddhist

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:14.750
you will face some discrimination's
in many ways in Myanmar.

00:20:14.750 --> 00:20:17.430
And so what I would like to
put here is that there is

00:20:18.330 --> 00:20:21.170
the concept flag and faith, right?

00:20:21.170 --> 00:20:25.000
This the Buddhist led military government

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:28.510
imagined the politics in Myanmar, India,

00:20:28.510 --> 00:20:30.230
Buddhist way of thinking.

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:31.460
That is what happened

00:20:31.460 --> 00:20:34.210
especially in this
post-independent period,

00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:36.810
what I'll call it, make
Buddhism great again.

00:20:36.810 --> 00:20:40.130
Which has been very,
very obvious in Myanmar.

00:20:40.130 --> 00:20:41.900
And then of course some of these,

00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:43.810
the military government
leaders are influenced

00:20:43.810 --> 00:20:45.480
by Donald Trump's slogan definitely.

00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:49.060
Make America great again, right?

00:20:49.060 --> 00:20:52.030
So they also use make
Buddhism great again,

00:20:52.030 --> 00:20:54.660
because they feel that
Buddhism was declined

00:20:54.660 --> 00:20:56.980
during the British colonial period.

00:20:56.980 --> 00:20:57.980
So that's how they feel.

00:20:57.980 --> 00:21:00.830
Now let's move to the third movement,

00:21:00.830 --> 00:21:03.130
what I will call it old
Buddhist Nationalism

00:21:03.130 --> 00:21:03.973
in the new coup.

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:07.260
In this movement, it's slightly different

00:21:07.260 --> 00:21:09.120
from the previous two movement.

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:13.364
In this movement it's not just
the ethnic identity conflict,

00:21:13.364 --> 00:21:16.550
but also the ideology conflict.

00:21:16.550 --> 00:21:19.280
That means the conflict
is not just between,

00:21:19.280 --> 00:21:23.330
the majority of them are Buddhist
and ethnic minority group.

00:21:23.330 --> 00:21:27.220
But among the Buddhist also
have some conflict, right?

00:21:27.220 --> 00:21:29.380
That is something
important to remember here,

00:21:29.380 --> 00:21:32.320
so Buddhism against
Buddhism that is obvious.

00:21:32.320 --> 00:21:33.620
And then leadership, dictatorship

00:21:33.620 --> 00:21:35.630
against democracy movement.

00:21:35.630 --> 00:21:38.170
As well as the Burma against the Burma.

00:21:38.170 --> 00:21:41.540
That means the conflict
becomes bigger than

00:21:41.540 --> 00:21:45.433
the second one and the third one.

00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:50.383
Let me touch on these
opportunities and challenges.

00:21:51.550 --> 00:21:53.230
The rise of this, the new coup,

00:21:53.230 --> 00:21:56.094
create the opportunity of inter-religious

00:21:56.094 --> 00:22:00.570
and inter-ethnic solidarity among these...

00:22:05.790 --> 00:22:07.320
Protestors.

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:08.153
If (indistinct).

00:22:10.819 --> 00:22:14.430
But in this current movement
people from different religions

00:22:14.430 --> 00:22:16.500
and different ethnic groups come together

00:22:16.500 --> 00:22:19.170
and share their
inter-religious solidarity,

00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:20.710
or maybe a unity.

00:22:20.710 --> 00:22:24.347
Of course, the question
is how deep that unity is?

00:22:24.347 --> 00:22:27.950
But at least we can see
that this the coup create

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:29.680
the opportunity of inter-religious

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:33.650
and inter-ethnic solidarity
for the democracy movement.

00:22:33.650 --> 00:22:36.210
Especially that movement is led by

00:22:36.210 --> 00:22:38.880
the generation Z, or maybe
the young generation.

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:41.030
You can see it in this picture.

00:22:41.030 --> 00:22:42.630
One on the left is (indistinct).

00:22:44.492 --> 00:22:49.310
And the right one is ethnically Shani...

00:22:49.310 --> 00:22:50.450
It is dharma.

00:22:50.450 --> 00:22:54.890
And so they just try to
bridge their religious

00:22:54.890 --> 00:22:58.570
and ethnic divides to
confront the military coup

00:22:58.570 --> 00:22:59.670
as their common enemy.

00:22:59.670 --> 00:23:01.193
This is something important to remember

00:23:01.193 --> 00:23:02.573
with this current movement.

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:06.243
So here is my question,
eight months into the coup,

00:23:06.243 --> 00:23:08.560
what is the state of democracy movement

00:23:09.610 --> 00:23:12.990
earlier Ma Thida and Tun
has covered a lot of things.

00:23:12.990 --> 00:23:14.830
And let me briefly touch on that

00:23:15.860 --> 00:23:18.330
from the perspective of
the ethnic minority issue.

00:23:18.330 --> 00:23:20.680
In my view, in these
eight months of the coup,

00:23:20.680 --> 00:23:23.000
there are two strategies
that the people are using

00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:24.609
today in Myanmar.

00:23:24.609 --> 00:23:26.910
First is a non-violence in the beginning,

00:23:26.910 --> 00:23:29.490
they protest on the street
in a non-violent way.

00:23:29.490 --> 00:23:33.660
But the military government
used a violent crackdown

00:23:33.660 --> 00:23:35.940
on the peaceful protesters.

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:38.550
And then maybe sometime in March,

00:23:38.550 --> 00:23:42.079
they start thinking about how they should

00:23:42.079 --> 00:23:44.650
themselves self-defense.

00:23:44.650 --> 00:23:49.293
So in that case they use a violent way,

00:23:49.293 --> 00:23:53.310
and especially in Mindat
where I'm from originally,

00:23:53.310 --> 00:23:56.580
they formed this PDF,
People Defense Force.

00:23:56.580 --> 00:24:00.210
Which has been changed to a CDF later on,

00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:03.440
to make their chin, this
resistant movement unique.

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:05.720
So that is something that we can see.

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:08.130
In this second strategy,

00:24:08.130 --> 00:24:12.470
we can also see that the way
the current movement is also

00:24:12.470 --> 00:24:16.780
trying to go beyond Aung San
Suu Kyi method of nonviolence.

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:18.790
And we don't know whether

00:24:18.790 --> 00:24:20.900
Aung San Suu Kyi might
agree with that or not,

00:24:20.900 --> 00:24:21.733
we don't know.

00:24:21.733 --> 00:24:23.090
But what we can say here is that

00:24:23.090 --> 00:24:26.820
the current movement is
trying to try to go beyond

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:29.573
Aung San Suu Kyi method of non-violence.

00:24:32.430 --> 00:24:34.260
- You have to stop soon David.

00:24:34.260 --> 00:24:36.320
- Yes, okay, here...

00:24:37.530 --> 00:24:39.303
Let me go here to the...

00:24:40.382 --> 00:24:41.215
To the third point.

00:24:41.215 --> 00:24:43.230
My question is, do the ethnic minorities

00:24:44.630 --> 00:24:46.910
and ethnic majorities
share the same visions?

00:24:46.910 --> 00:24:48.150
I think the answer is yes,

00:24:48.150 --> 00:24:50.150
but which they can further argue that

00:24:50.150 --> 00:24:53.390
the ethnic majority have a
vision of freedom from the coup.

00:24:53.390 --> 00:24:56.233
But the ethnic minority
have a double visions,

00:24:57.120 --> 00:24:59.060
freedom not only from the coup,

00:24:59.060 --> 00:25:01.170
but also from the Buddhist nationalism.

00:25:01.170 --> 00:25:03.270
That is something significant.

00:25:03.270 --> 00:25:05.550
And let me move to the last point.

00:25:05.550 --> 00:25:07.780
Does Myanmar have the future of democracy?

00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:08.620
If no, why?

00:25:08.620 --> 00:25:12.650
And if yes, how?

00:25:12.650 --> 00:25:14.991
We might have different obedience,

00:25:14.991 --> 00:25:16.450
but in my view, yes, the answer is yes.

00:25:16.450 --> 00:25:17.910
But we need two movement,

00:25:17.910 --> 00:25:19.960
not only internal movement in Myanmar,

00:25:19.960 --> 00:25:21.870
but also the external movement.

00:25:21.870 --> 00:25:23.560
And international communities

00:25:23.560 --> 00:25:25.633
cooperating with internal movement.

00:25:26.648 --> 00:25:29.384
In that case, I will urge
the international community

00:25:29.384 --> 00:25:31.387
to recognize NUG.

00:25:31.387 --> 00:25:34.330
And second, I would urge
the international community

00:25:34.330 --> 00:25:37.640
to advocate for freedom of
Myanmar people in many ways.

00:25:37.640 --> 00:25:41.300
And third to financially
support the CDM strikers,

00:25:41.300 --> 00:25:42.400
and IDP people.

00:25:42.400 --> 00:25:46.840
And I believe that the next
big US ambassador Scot Marciel,

00:25:46.840 --> 00:25:49.240
will talk a little bit
more about this issue.

00:25:49.240 --> 00:25:50.073
Thank you.

00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:52.050
- Thank you, David.

00:25:53.720 --> 00:25:56.990
Next, let me recognize Scot Marciel,

00:25:56.990 --> 00:25:59.940
a career of Foreign Service Officer.

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:04.940
Who has spent most of his
career in Southeast Asia,

00:26:05.280 --> 00:26:08.600
was ambassador to Indonesia,
ambassador to Burma,

00:26:08.600 --> 00:26:10.590
and ambassador to ASEAN.

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:12.783
Scot, you have seven minutes.

00:26:16.320 --> 00:26:17.993
- Thanks very much.

00:26:17.993 --> 00:26:21.420
And thanks for letting me join this panel

00:26:21.420 --> 00:26:24.490
as sort of the outsider
among the four speakers,

00:26:24.490 --> 00:26:27.580
since the other three,
obviously are from Myanmar,

00:26:27.580 --> 00:26:29.060
and I'm very clearly not.

00:26:29.060 --> 00:26:32.143
But have had the pleasure
of spending some time there.

00:26:33.688 --> 00:26:37.880
I'd like to frame my comments a little bit

00:26:37.880 --> 00:26:40.723
in a big picture policy way.

00:26:41.870 --> 00:26:44.510
The way I look at this situation,

00:26:44.510 --> 00:26:47.310
the Myanmar military, the Tatmadaw.

00:26:47.310 --> 00:26:52.240
As we know, around the
country from 1962 to 2010, 11,

00:26:52.240 --> 00:26:56.750
before it started loosening
up and allowing reforms.

00:26:56.750 --> 00:26:58.933
And in that roughly 50 year period,

00:26:59.920 --> 00:27:02.490
they ran the country about as badly

00:27:02.490 --> 00:27:06.080
as one can run a country in every respect.

00:27:06.080 --> 00:27:09.580
Obviously there's rightly
been a huge amount of focus

00:27:09.580 --> 00:27:12.520
on the conflict and
human rights violations,

00:27:12.520 --> 00:27:14.890
and lack of freedom and democracy.

00:27:14.890 --> 00:27:18.730
But they also managed to drive
the economy into the ground,

00:27:18.730 --> 00:27:22.350
while destroying or nearly
destroying the health system,

00:27:22.350 --> 00:27:24.160
the education system.

00:27:24.160 --> 00:27:26.890
And actually exacerbating
the very ethnic conflicts

00:27:26.890 --> 00:27:30.883
which David mentioned that the
rule was supposed to resolve.

00:27:32.133 --> 00:27:33.890
You have that history,

00:27:33.890 --> 00:27:37.000
and then you had the
period of roughly a decade

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:40.210
when the reforms began.

00:27:40.210 --> 00:27:43.720
From when the reforms began in 2011, 12,

00:27:43.720 --> 00:27:45.790
until the coup this year.

00:27:45.790 --> 00:27:50.790
And in that period you
had a lot of reform.

00:27:50.880 --> 00:27:54.400
You had significant increases in freedom,

00:27:54.400 --> 00:27:59.400
you had a near consensus
on the need to move

00:27:59.400 --> 00:28:02.163
toward a federal democracy.

00:28:03.310 --> 00:28:07.390
In the period I was there, 2015 to 2020,

00:28:07.390 --> 00:28:10.130
a lot of us focused and a lot
of the media attention focused

00:28:10.130 --> 00:28:13.590
on the areas of continuing
disagreement and problem,

00:28:13.590 --> 00:28:15.840
the Rohingya crisis.

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:17.290
The continuing conflict

00:28:17.290 --> 00:28:20.240
and the lack of movement
on the peace process.

00:28:20.240 --> 00:28:22.690
Continuing problems with lack of

00:28:22.690 --> 00:28:26.000
full freedoms and human rights.

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:28.430
The military's continuing
human rights violations,

00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:29.960
and so on.

00:28:29.960 --> 00:28:31.550
But when I look back at it now,

00:28:31.550 --> 00:28:33.330
all of those things were very real.

00:28:33.330 --> 00:28:36.030
And there was very serious
challenges that the country

00:28:37.590 --> 00:28:40.310
was confronting and still has to confront.

00:28:40.310 --> 00:28:43.310
But in my view, there was

00:28:43.310 --> 00:28:47.460
near consensus in the
country on one thing,

00:28:47.460 --> 00:28:51.860
that the country had an
opportunity to move forward.

00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:55.730
And it was moving forward
in fits and starts.

00:28:55.730 --> 00:28:59.730
And everybody, just about everybody,

00:28:59.730 --> 00:29:03.270
was adamant that the one thing
that they did not want to see

00:29:03.270 --> 00:29:06.613
was a return to the bad
old days of military rule.

00:29:07.920 --> 00:29:11.570
And so the coup on February 1st,

00:29:11.570 --> 00:29:15.290
in addition to being completely
unjustified, illegitimate,

00:29:15.290 --> 00:29:16.123
you name it.

00:29:18.793 --> 00:29:22.300
It basically met instant opposition

00:29:22.300 --> 00:29:24.253
from almost the entire country.

00:29:25.499 --> 00:29:26.900
Because of course, people
didn't want to have

00:29:26.900 --> 00:29:30.270
their freedoms taken
away and their democracy,

00:29:30.270 --> 00:29:32.580
flawed as it might have been, taken away.

00:29:32.580 --> 00:29:36.587
But also I think because
people saw their hope

00:29:36.587 --> 00:29:41.580
and their opportunity for a
better future being taken away.

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:46.580
And I think this helps explain
the incredibly widespread

00:29:48.240 --> 00:29:51.170
resistance, peaceful and otherwise,

00:29:51.170 --> 00:29:53.840
to a return to military rule.

00:29:53.840 --> 00:29:55.190
This is not...

00:29:56.210 --> 00:29:58.680
Some opposition movement,

00:29:58.680 --> 00:30:01.593
this is really the country of Myanmar.

00:30:02.490 --> 00:30:06.850
Ethnic Burma and ethnic
minorities pretty much together,

00:30:06.850 --> 00:30:10.300
united in, what's in effect,
a national resistance

00:30:10.300 --> 00:30:12.360
against military rule.

00:30:12.360 --> 00:30:14.020
That is, I view,

00:30:14.020 --> 00:30:17.060
is largely the equivalent
of occupying force.

00:30:17.060 --> 00:30:19.330
In the sense that they have no legitimacy,

00:30:19.330 --> 00:30:21.043
no popular support,

00:30:21.990 --> 00:30:26.990
no real interest in helping
people live better lives.

00:30:27.450 --> 00:30:29.990
It's striking the Tatmadaw
doesn't really care

00:30:29.990 --> 00:30:31.243
how much people suffer.

00:30:32.300 --> 00:30:33.990
It's almost unimaginable,

00:30:33.990 --> 00:30:36.560
they not only impose their own suffering,

00:30:36.560 --> 00:30:40.120
but weaponize COVID
and take other measures

00:30:40.120 --> 00:30:41.690
that make it clear that they don't give a

00:30:41.690 --> 00:30:44.460
damn about the people at all.

00:30:44.460 --> 00:30:46.193
It's all about power for them.

00:30:49.150 --> 00:30:50.323
Going forward,

00:30:51.360 --> 00:30:55.763
I don't see the resistance
to the coup waning.

00:30:56.730 --> 00:30:58.940
If anything, it will increase both, again,

00:30:58.940 --> 00:31:01.463
peaceful means and non peaceful means.

00:31:02.440 --> 00:31:04.130
The Tatmadaw given its history,

00:31:04.130 --> 00:31:08.330
is unlikely to say, "Okay, well,

00:31:08.330 --> 00:31:09.610
we'll just give up."

00:31:09.610 --> 00:31:13.420
So sadly, I think, that
means for some time

00:31:13.420 --> 00:31:18.270
we're going to see more
suffering, more violence.

00:31:18.270 --> 00:31:20.930
And it is really important,
I think people are aware,

00:31:20.930 --> 00:31:23.900
it is really important to
remember and to think every day

00:31:23.900 --> 00:31:27.410
about the incredible
amount of human suffering

00:31:27.410 --> 00:31:30.280
that's going on as a result of this coup

00:31:30.280 --> 00:31:32.120
throughout the country.

00:31:32.120 --> 00:31:35.700
The violence for sure,
people suffering from COVID

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:40.700
and not being able to get
adequate medical help.

00:31:40.920 --> 00:31:44.610
People being displaced,
people losing their jobs,

00:31:44.610 --> 00:31:45.733
going hungry.

00:31:47.445 --> 00:31:49.930
Across the board it's just

00:31:49.930 --> 00:31:52.840
a horrific humanitarian situation.

00:31:52.840 --> 00:31:55.820
And I think it's important that the world

00:31:55.820 --> 00:31:57.400
stay very focused on that.

00:31:57.400 --> 00:32:00.439
And remember for this is not
just a political problem,

00:32:00.439 --> 00:32:03.927
it's a problem of human,
it's a humanitarian crisis

00:32:03.927 --> 00:32:05.713
and a problem of human suffering.

00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:09.960
Going forward, looking
for a little bit further,

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:12.720
there's been this discussion or debate

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:15.640
about whether dialogue is needed.

00:32:15.640 --> 00:32:17.290
Whether dialogue is appropriate

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:18.900
to try to resolve this problem.

00:32:18.900 --> 00:32:20.490
And it's really tricky,

00:32:20.490 --> 00:32:22.270
I think it's really
important to be careful

00:32:22.270 --> 00:32:24.620
how we talk about this.

00:32:24.620 --> 00:32:27.150
Because neither side is unlikely

00:32:27.150 --> 00:32:29.982
to just give up or surrender.

00:32:29.982 --> 00:32:32.460
At some point, probably,

00:32:32.460 --> 00:32:35.130
there will be a need for
some kind of discussions

00:32:35.130 --> 00:32:37.650
to hopefully find a way out of this.

00:32:37.650 --> 00:32:41.000
But I think what's key is
that the conditions now

00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:43.420
are not right for that.

00:32:43.420 --> 00:32:46.600
The Junta's has shown no
interest in discussions,

00:32:46.600 --> 00:32:48.730
they've completely ignored

00:32:48.730 --> 00:32:52.560
and blown off ASEAN efforts to mediate.

00:32:52.560 --> 00:32:55.490
They've shown no interest in this at all.

00:32:55.490 --> 00:32:58.270
And my sense is that a lot of the people,

00:32:58.270 --> 00:32:59.670
a lot of Myanmar people,

00:32:59.670 --> 00:33:03.160
are not interested in these
discussions at this point now.

00:33:03.160 --> 00:33:07.880
I think the hope for me for the future,

00:33:07.880 --> 00:33:12.880
my belief is that what's
needed is for more pressure

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:14.520
on the Junta,

00:33:14.520 --> 00:33:17.173
domestic pressure and
international pressure.

00:33:18.110 --> 00:33:19.490
I don't believe they're going to be able

00:33:19.490 --> 00:33:22.420
to restore stability and be
able to govern the country.

00:33:22.420 --> 00:33:23.253
Okay?

00:33:23.253 --> 00:33:26.740
And I think that what's needed is

00:33:26.740 --> 00:33:29.500
intensive pressure over
a long period of time.

00:33:29.500 --> 00:33:31.570
So that more and more military officers

00:33:31.570 --> 00:33:34.320
understand that they
have no hope of winning.

00:33:34.320 --> 00:33:36.400
And at that point they may be willing

00:33:36.400 --> 00:33:39.280
to look for a way out
and make concessions,

00:33:39.280 --> 00:33:42.640
which then could lead to
the beginning of dialogue.

00:33:42.640 --> 00:33:43.473
I'll stop there.

00:33:44.950 --> 00:33:46.220
- Thank you, Scot.

00:33:46.220 --> 00:33:49.050
I wanted to add a couple of things just

00:33:49.050 --> 00:33:52.023
into the mix for the
subsequent discussion.

00:33:53.120 --> 00:33:58.100
The thing that does strike
me is that the Burma are now

00:33:58.100 --> 00:34:01.360
suffering a form of...

00:34:01.360 --> 00:34:04.370
Direct violence and oppression,

00:34:04.370 --> 00:34:06.830
that the ethnic minorities
have been suffering

00:34:06.830 --> 00:34:08.723
from since 1962.

00:34:09.870 --> 00:34:13.570
And it's now come, if you
like, to the Burmese heartland.

00:34:13.570 --> 00:34:16.023
And I, as a political scientist,

00:34:17.240 --> 00:34:21.300
I don't know actually of another regime,

00:34:21.300 --> 00:34:26.300
another military, that is
so universally despised.

00:34:26.970 --> 00:34:30.310
This is not a rebellion by students

00:34:30.310 --> 00:34:32.387
and cosmopolitan elites in the cities.

00:34:32.387 --> 00:34:37.123
This is a rebellion by the
entire population; In villages,

00:34:38.312 --> 00:34:40.820
rural villages.

00:34:40.820 --> 00:34:43.057
And ethnic minority areas.

00:34:43.057 --> 00:34:46.950
It is such a completely predatory regime

00:34:46.950 --> 00:34:50.247
that has no popular base
and no popular support.

00:34:50.247 --> 00:34:55.003
That's in a extraordinary
situation that in the long run,

00:34:56.350 --> 00:35:00.090
this is a regime that
governs by sheer repression,

00:35:00.090 --> 00:35:01.503
violence and terror.

00:35:02.530 --> 00:35:07.139
And the interesting thing to me is that

00:35:07.139 --> 00:35:08.389
the Tatmadaw...

00:35:09.270 --> 00:35:12.390
Because it has its own education system,

00:35:12.390 --> 00:35:13.850
its own...

00:35:15.820 --> 00:35:17.530
Supply chain,

00:35:17.530 --> 00:35:19.820
it has its own healthcare system,

00:35:19.820 --> 00:35:22.150
it has its own pensions.

00:35:22.150 --> 00:35:25.410
It insists that people
stay on the barracks.

00:35:25.410 --> 00:35:30.410
It is probably the most
insulated military...

00:35:30.640 --> 00:35:31.980
That the world has seen.

00:35:31.980 --> 00:35:34.697
There may be a couple of other
examples a little like it,

00:35:34.697 --> 00:35:37.210
but what's extraordinary
is that you could say

00:35:37.210 --> 00:35:41.070
that the Tatmadaw does
not really live in Burma.

00:35:41.070 --> 00:35:43.343
That is to say they are so insulated.

00:35:44.850 --> 00:35:48.040
The horrific question is that,

00:35:48.040 --> 00:35:52.600
that insulation insulates it
to some considerable degree.

00:35:52.600 --> 00:35:54.820
How much, I don't know,

00:35:54.820 --> 00:35:57.650
from internal defections, from a split,

00:35:57.650 --> 00:36:00.110
and the officer core, and so on.

00:36:00.110 --> 00:36:03.050
It looks, although there are defections,

00:36:03.050 --> 00:36:04.990
there's no evidence that
you're going to have

00:36:04.990 --> 00:36:09.330
a split in the military which
would lead to a situation,

00:36:09.330 --> 00:36:12.793
I think, of perhaps more radical change.

00:36:13.960 --> 00:36:15.340
I just wanted to put that on the table.

00:36:15.340 --> 00:36:19.730
I wanted to open it both for let's add,

00:36:19.730 --> 00:36:21.750
let's have the participants...

00:36:23.840 --> 00:36:25.410
Chime in with their questions.

00:36:25.410 --> 00:36:27.200
If your question is,

00:36:27.200 --> 00:36:29.980
it would be good if your
question were directed

00:36:30.920 --> 00:36:33.640
to one or another of the panelists.

00:36:33.640 --> 00:36:36.290
Because then we would save
a good deal of the time,

00:36:36.290 --> 00:36:38.567
rather than having four people answer.

00:36:39.587 --> 00:36:43.460
I'll leave it to Chris
or to Erik to recognize

00:36:44.630 --> 00:36:46.100
members of the audience

00:36:48.500 --> 00:36:49.450
who have questions.

00:36:51.520 --> 00:36:52.740
Can we have that again?

00:36:52.740 --> 00:36:53.702
- [Announcer] There's
a question in the chat.

00:36:53.702 --> 00:36:55.830
- Yeah, I see it.

00:36:55.830 --> 00:36:57.330
Shall I address that question?

00:36:58.490 --> 00:36:59.900
- Please.

00:36:59.900 --> 00:37:02.910
- The question is how do you
think the role of ASEAN leaders

00:37:02.910 --> 00:37:05.823
in coping, helping the
current situation in Myanmar.

00:37:07.440 --> 00:37:10.910
I think you have within ASEAN

00:37:10.910 --> 00:37:13.400
some governments that
seem extremely concerned

00:37:13.400 --> 00:37:15.840
about the situation and
have been quite outspoken.

00:37:15.840 --> 00:37:19.630
And then you have others
who have been less so,

00:37:19.630 --> 00:37:22.650
so ASEAN seem divided.

00:37:22.650 --> 00:37:25.320
I think you saw that
early effort back in April

00:37:26.830 --> 00:37:30.490
to have this emergency meeting.

00:37:30.490 --> 00:37:32.720
Which they came up with five points,

00:37:32.720 --> 00:37:33.910
supposedly of agreement.

00:37:33.910 --> 00:37:37.107
Although the Junta immediately
rejected them (chuckles),

00:37:38.810 --> 00:37:41.470
within a day or two, I
believe, of the agreement.

00:37:41.470 --> 00:37:43.310
And since then, I think ASEAN struggled

00:37:43.310 --> 00:37:44.963
to find a way forward.

00:37:47.220 --> 00:37:49.033
This is really harmful for ASEAN,

00:37:50.030 --> 00:37:53.600
in addition to the suffering
of the Myanmar people.

00:37:53.600 --> 00:37:56.120
Myanmar once again has returned to being

00:37:56.120 --> 00:37:59.860
a really black mark for ASEAN,

00:37:59.860 --> 00:38:03.610
as one of its members is
behaving in this horrific...

00:38:03.610 --> 00:38:04.640
It's the military at least

00:38:04.640 --> 00:38:06.530
behaving in this horrific fashion.

00:38:06.530 --> 00:38:08.940
And I think ASEAN is really struggling

00:38:09.850 --> 00:38:10.683
with how to deal with it.

00:38:10.683 --> 00:38:14.670
They don't have really
measures, course of measures,

00:38:14.670 --> 00:38:18.640
to employ to pressure Myanmar that much.

00:38:18.640 --> 00:38:21.120
Their approaches normally one to try to

00:38:21.120 --> 00:38:25.150
use the ASEAN family relationship

00:38:25.150 --> 00:38:28.610
to try to win trust and have quiet talks,

00:38:28.610 --> 00:38:29.803
and try to nudge.

00:38:30.720 --> 00:38:31.560
But...

00:38:33.706 --> 00:38:35.220
I don't know that anyone's ever succeeded

00:38:35.220 --> 00:38:38.490
in quietly nudging this
military, the Tatmadaw.

00:38:38.490 --> 00:38:41.993
They're not nudgeable, if that's a word.

00:38:43.230 --> 00:38:47.040
I do think it's been a
little bit disappointing,

00:38:47.040 --> 00:38:48.290
but I think it's important that

00:38:48.290 --> 00:38:51.160
the ASEAN members keep pushing.

00:38:51.160 --> 00:38:53.280
Because their own organization

00:38:53.280 --> 00:38:55.070
is being undermined by what happens.

00:38:55.070 --> 00:38:59.730
As well as the stability of
the region is being undermined

00:38:59.730 --> 00:39:01.870
by the Junta and this coup.

00:39:01.870 --> 00:39:05.760
I hope the ASEAN leaders
will keep looking for ways

00:39:05.760 --> 00:39:09.403
to push the generals to rethink this.

00:39:10.630 --> 00:39:11.473
- Next question.

00:39:13.660 --> 00:39:15.610
- We have a question from (indistinct),

00:39:16.840 --> 00:39:19.600
and then after that, there's
a question in the chat.

00:39:19.600 --> 00:39:21.420
There's actually two
questions in the chat,

00:39:21.420 --> 00:39:23.590
but they turn out to be the same person.

00:39:23.590 --> 00:39:26.210
One came via email and one into the chat.

00:39:26.210 --> 00:39:29.120
So I say we take the question
from (indistinct) first,

00:39:29.120 --> 00:39:31.173
and then the question from the chat.

00:39:36.419 --> 00:39:37.252
- Thank you Erik,

00:39:37.252 --> 00:39:39.650
and thank you all for your comments.

00:39:39.650 --> 00:39:41.807
I was going to type the
question in the chat box,

00:39:41.807 --> 00:39:43.950
but I will state it here.

00:39:43.950 --> 00:39:47.200
My question is directed to Dr Martina.

00:39:47.200 --> 00:39:51.501
And it has to do with why does
the military of the regime,

00:39:51.501 --> 00:39:53.930
(indistinct) in particular
as you've described,

00:39:53.930 --> 00:39:56.260
bother with law at all.

00:39:56.260 --> 00:39:58.820
When killing can be arbitrary

00:39:58.820 --> 00:40:00.330
and the regime is governed by,

00:40:00.330 --> 00:40:04.300
as Professor Scott
described, sheer repression,

00:40:04.300 --> 00:40:06.278
violent and terror.

00:40:06.278 --> 00:40:09.033
Why bother with law at all?

00:40:10.110 --> 00:40:11.223
Thank you so much.

00:40:12.690 --> 00:40:17.330
- Well, the concept behind forming,

00:40:17.330 --> 00:40:21.490
or recruiting the soldiers
into the Myanmar Tatmadaw

00:40:21.490 --> 00:40:24.720
has so many problems already.

00:40:24.720 --> 00:40:29.180
The incentives to become the
military officer or the soldier

00:40:29.180 --> 00:40:31.920
is not to protect the people.

00:40:31.920 --> 00:40:33.420
That's very clear.

00:40:33.420 --> 00:40:38.050
It's just to make them
having a privileged positions

00:40:38.050 --> 00:40:41.780
among the others,
especially above citizens.

00:40:41.780 --> 00:40:45.610
So the concept of the
Tatmadaw and the soldiers,

00:40:45.610 --> 00:40:47.877
and military officer, is they...

00:40:50.060 --> 00:40:52.840
Justify people...

00:40:52.840 --> 00:40:56.702
To govern or to bully the citizens.

00:40:56.702 --> 00:41:01.660
That's made more clear after the coup.

00:41:01.660 --> 00:41:04.600
And after the coup the,

00:41:04.600 --> 00:41:06.933
because of the economic sanctions,

00:41:06.933 --> 00:41:10.230
and the other disobedience
from the citizens.

00:41:10.230 --> 00:41:14.550
Military ISERP have not much income

00:41:14.550 --> 00:41:16.590
to give

00:41:16.590 --> 00:41:19.780
enough support to it's subordinates.

00:41:19.780 --> 00:41:22.430
So especially on the ground, the soldiers,

00:41:22.430 --> 00:41:24.890
are hunger.

00:41:24.890 --> 00:41:27.570
And for that reason...

00:41:27.570 --> 00:41:30.330
And then the rule of law never being

00:41:30.330 --> 00:41:32.740
the issue for the military leaders.

00:41:32.740 --> 00:41:36.170
That's they just let them
do whatever the arbitrary...

00:41:38.230 --> 00:41:41.920
So much inhumane things to do.

00:41:41.920 --> 00:41:46.500
And then I think another
thing is since the February,

00:41:46.500 --> 00:41:50.070
there was no single day...

00:41:50.070 --> 00:41:51.120
To get rest

00:41:51.120 --> 00:41:53.440
for the soldiers and army men.

00:41:53.440 --> 00:41:57.720
Because of either the peaceful protest,

00:41:57.720 --> 00:42:00.680
or the UMP struggle is
going on everywhere,

00:42:00.680 --> 00:42:01.730
all over the country.

00:42:01.730 --> 00:42:04.220
This was the very hard for them,

00:42:04.220 --> 00:42:09.220
for the nonstop action for
more than seven, eight months.

00:42:10.430 --> 00:42:14.500
In that case, they just...

00:42:15.680 --> 00:42:16.770
In...

00:42:18.970 --> 00:42:20.070
The...

00:42:20.070 --> 00:42:22.630
Deep bitterness and angers,

00:42:22.630 --> 00:42:26.090
and they cannot think
of the better solutions

00:42:26.090 --> 00:42:30.510
rather than making as much
as forces onto the citizens

00:42:30.510 --> 00:42:33.182
to get cooling down.

00:42:33.182 --> 00:42:37.130
Or to get afraid of doing
anymore, something like that.

00:42:37.130 --> 00:42:38.770
So that one case.

00:42:38.770 --> 00:42:42.930
And another case is they are
somehow addicted to either

00:42:42.930 --> 00:42:47.850
alcohol or the drugs, the
methamphetamine by themselves,

00:42:47.850 --> 00:42:50.320
the military soldiers and
the military officers.

00:42:50.320 --> 00:42:53.550
That's why they are inhumane activities

00:42:53.550 --> 00:42:54.910
is more or less related to.

00:42:54.910 --> 00:42:58.000
That's why we have been
noticing a lot of the

00:42:58.000 --> 00:43:03.000
fighting among each other,
after too much drinking alcohol.

00:43:03.200 --> 00:43:08.160
The soldiers killed it's a...

00:43:08.160 --> 00:43:10.550
Military officer, that's going on.

00:43:10.550 --> 00:43:11.970
So I think the...

00:43:11.970 --> 00:43:16.970
And then there will be the keep
ongoing the impunity issue,

00:43:18.320 --> 00:43:20.080
the hiring military officer.

00:43:20.080 --> 00:43:22.200
They never ever take any action

00:43:22.200 --> 00:43:25.150
against their subordinates whatsoever.

00:43:25.150 --> 00:43:26.360
Whatever they do, they do.

00:43:26.360 --> 00:43:30.070
That's why the ransom
cases is almost like,

00:43:30.070 --> 00:43:31.880
not just betrayed the citizens,

00:43:31.880 --> 00:43:34.980
but also betraying itself,
the Tatmadaw itself.

00:43:34.980 --> 00:43:38.070
They never let the military officer know,

00:43:38.070 --> 00:43:43.070
but on the ground, they trying
to get as much as possible

00:43:43.410 --> 00:43:47.710
from the Federalist members
of the arrested revolutionist.

00:43:48.900 --> 00:43:51.030
That's how they get our money

00:43:51.030 --> 00:43:53.250
during this kind of desperate period.

00:43:53.250 --> 00:43:56.890
This is the period of
desperate for them too,

00:43:56.890 --> 00:44:00.063
they cannot get rest, they
need to do something else.

00:44:03.100 --> 00:44:06.350
Also they need to feed
for their family members,

00:44:06.350 --> 00:44:08.543
and where else they can get money?

00:44:08.543 --> 00:44:11.910
Their salary is pretty much granted

00:44:11.910 --> 00:44:14.920
by the military regime too.

00:44:14.920 --> 00:44:17.313
It's like this vicious cycle.

00:44:18.758 --> 00:44:20.753
- I just wanted to add to that.

00:44:23.570 --> 00:44:28.450
The pattern of absolute predatory robbery,

00:44:28.450 --> 00:44:30.150
ransom, and so on,

00:44:30.150 --> 00:44:32.690
much of which has to do with,

00:44:32.690 --> 00:44:36.250
it's just the military
turned loose to make money

00:44:36.250 --> 00:44:41.250
by kidnapping people and holding
their families, and so on.

00:44:41.490 --> 00:44:43.610
I thought the question, however,

00:44:43.610 --> 00:44:47.103
is worth pondering and I don't
have quite the answer to it.

00:44:47.103 --> 00:44:50.530
And that's the question
of why did they bother

00:44:50.530 --> 00:44:52.270
with this patina

00:44:52.270 --> 00:44:53.980
with this fog

00:44:53.980 --> 00:44:56.240
of legality?

00:44:56.240 --> 00:44:59.820
The fact is that people were
charged under section 5-0-5.

00:44:59.820 --> 00:45:00.653
- Yes.

00:45:02.398 --> 00:45:04.780
- What's interesting to me is that

00:45:04.780 --> 00:45:07.200
they do whatever they want to do.

00:45:07.200 --> 00:45:09.280
But they require,

00:45:09.280 --> 00:45:13.930
and it shows us the
legitimacy of the rule of law.

00:45:13.930 --> 00:45:18.270
At some level, they have
to have this curtain,

00:45:18.270 --> 00:45:22.120
in which it appears that there's
a section of the legal code

00:45:22.120 --> 00:45:23.040
that they are using.

00:45:23.040 --> 00:45:25.453
They have lawyers, they have judges.

00:45:26.671 --> 00:45:31.170
And the whole point of a
successful dictatorship today

00:45:31.170 --> 00:45:35.530
is to have the institutional
facade of a democracy,

00:45:35.530 --> 00:45:40.530
including elections,
judges, and legal codes.

00:45:40.920 --> 00:45:43.820
And to under that cover,
do whatever you want.

00:45:43.820 --> 00:45:48.750
But it shows us actually
that behind all of this,

00:45:48.750 --> 00:45:53.670
there's a tremendous
legitimacy of the rule of law,

00:45:53.670 --> 00:45:55.663
and of democratic governance.

00:45:56.860 --> 00:46:00.253
Because they have to fake it constantly.

00:46:01.670 --> 00:46:03.430
Yes, next question.

00:46:03.430 --> 00:46:06.993
The one was in the chat, I gather Erik.

00:46:09.150 --> 00:46:11.070
- Yeah, Jim, thank you.

00:46:11.070 --> 00:46:13.860
There's a series of questions

00:46:13.860 --> 00:46:18.860
that congeal around the US
state department's position

00:46:19.630 --> 00:46:21.530
on violence in Myanmar.

00:46:21.530 --> 00:46:23.650
I'd like to broaden that
question just a little bit,

00:46:23.650 --> 00:46:26.410
so that it's not only
directed to the ambassador,

00:46:26.410 --> 00:46:28.473
but it's clearly on people's mind.

00:46:30.340 --> 00:46:32.887
Tun had also mentioned the idea of,

00:46:32.887 --> 00:46:35.110
"Is there a case of just war, right?

00:46:35.110 --> 00:46:36.750
Or just violence."

00:46:36.750 --> 00:46:40.170
And so, obviously the
questions would like to hear

00:46:40.170 --> 00:46:42.940
what ambassador Scot Marciel has to say

00:46:42.940 --> 00:46:45.190
about US position on that.

00:46:45.190 --> 00:46:50.040
Which is maybe a tall task to
announce in a panel like this.

00:46:50.040 --> 00:46:54.540
But I do think the topic of
violence when it's just, or not,

00:46:54.540 --> 00:46:57.430
deserves some attention by the panel.

00:46:57.430 --> 00:46:59.500
If all of you can reflect on that briefly,

00:46:59.500 --> 00:47:00.900
I think that would be great.

00:47:02.920 --> 00:47:03.753
- Scot?

00:47:05.520 --> 00:47:07.170
- Thanks.

00:47:07.170 --> 00:47:10.880
I'll try to address it
with a bit of a caveat.

00:47:10.880 --> 00:47:14.690
First, I want to stress
it's former ambassador,

00:47:14.690 --> 00:47:18.850
so I am not the ambassador to Myanmar.

00:47:18.850 --> 00:47:22.920
And what I'm going to say is
absolutely my personal views,

00:47:22.920 --> 00:47:26.180
it does not represent the views,

00:47:26.180 --> 00:47:29.280
necessarily represent the
views of the US government,

00:47:29.280 --> 00:47:31.010
or the state department.

00:47:31.010 --> 00:47:31.890
I think...

00:47:33.480 --> 00:47:37.647
First, it would be very
difficult for the US government

00:47:37.647 --> 00:47:40.087
and many other governments
to come out and say,

00:47:40.087 --> 00:47:41.720
"We support violence."

00:47:41.720 --> 00:47:42.580
It's just...

00:47:43.721 --> 00:47:46.570
And the way I look at this is that...

00:47:48.800 --> 00:47:52.870
The military created this
situation with the coup,

00:47:52.870 --> 00:47:57.860
and then when people tried
the peaceful approach

00:47:57.860 --> 00:48:02.040
of civil disobedience and
peaceful protests, and so on.

00:48:02.040 --> 00:48:06.793
The military responded with
horrific violence, as we know.

00:48:08.420 --> 00:48:10.280
One can argue that people weren't really

00:48:10.280 --> 00:48:12.780
left with the option of either dialogue

00:48:12.780 --> 00:48:14.040
or a peaceful protest.

00:48:14.040 --> 00:48:17.470
And so it's inevitable that some people

00:48:17.470 --> 00:48:21.020
have taken other measures.

00:48:21.020 --> 00:48:25.830
I think it's, again, it's
quite difficult for the

00:48:25.830 --> 00:48:27.600
US government or other
governments, I think,

00:48:27.600 --> 00:48:31.030
to come out and say we support violence.

00:48:31.030 --> 00:48:34.040
But I think if you looked at
the totality of statements

00:48:34.040 --> 00:48:37.010
from the US government
and the state department,

00:48:37.010 --> 00:48:40.390
it's very clear that
they place responsibility

00:48:40.390 --> 00:48:42.950
for what's happened on the military Junta.

00:48:42.950 --> 00:48:47.171
And are very much in support of...

00:48:47.171 --> 00:48:51.540
Of the people, and of efforts to restore,

00:48:51.540 --> 00:48:52.790
I wouldn't even say restore.

00:48:52.790 --> 00:48:57.240
Efforts to move the country
back onto a democratic path

00:48:57.240 --> 00:49:01.483
with a government that respects
people and human rights.

00:49:04.440 --> 00:49:05.940
- Anyone else want to comment?

00:49:06.780 --> 00:49:10.330
- Well, I just want to add one point.

00:49:10.330 --> 00:49:12.210
That's why I think from
the very beginning,

00:49:12.210 --> 00:49:14.130
the people of Myanmar requesting

00:49:14.130 --> 00:49:17.940
for having the heart to
be the responsibility,

00:49:17.940 --> 00:49:20.170
to protect and that sense.

00:49:20.170 --> 00:49:23.330
Still, I have so much concern about

00:49:23.330 --> 00:49:25.477
the transition of justice
for the longer run.

00:49:25.477 --> 00:49:29.662
For that reasons, we
seriously do have a presence

00:49:29.662 --> 00:49:34.490
of the third party security
forces on the ground.

00:49:34.490 --> 00:49:37.860
Otherwise there will be a non-stoppable

00:49:38.885 --> 00:49:41.650
revanche conflict going on,

00:49:41.650 --> 00:49:43.917
and that will be the end of our country.

00:49:43.917 --> 00:49:46.480
For that reason I think the trust between

00:49:46.480 --> 00:49:49.383
the current military and trust...

00:49:49.383 --> 00:49:51.750
The current military and
the people of Myanmar

00:49:51.750 --> 00:49:53.350
has already been finished.

00:49:53.350 --> 00:49:57.950
It cannot be built again,
it's ever finished already.

00:49:57.950 --> 00:50:00.550
That's why we seriously do

00:50:00.550 --> 00:50:03.440
need the third party

00:50:03.440 --> 00:50:06.870
security forces to...

00:50:06.870 --> 00:50:10.590
Reestablish the rule of law
inside the country without them,

00:50:10.590 --> 00:50:13.570
I think that's why we don't want any

00:50:13.570 --> 00:50:16.120
of the governments in the war

00:50:16.120 --> 00:50:18.480
to support the violence inside.

00:50:18.480 --> 00:50:22.840
We want the international
community to help us

00:50:22.840 --> 00:50:25.820
save Myanmar in terms of

00:50:25.820 --> 00:50:27.740
making the

00:50:27.740 --> 00:50:30.240
physical and literal...

00:50:31.640 --> 00:50:34.440
Protection for the sake of the people.

00:50:34.440 --> 00:50:37.580
The lives of the people, the
properties of the people.

00:50:40.950 --> 00:50:44.550
Either way, it might be
through the UN platform,

00:50:44.550 --> 00:50:45.383
or any way.

00:50:45.383 --> 00:50:48.660
But, to be frank, because of this failure

00:50:48.660 --> 00:50:52.640
a lot of our people have lost their trust

00:50:52.640 --> 00:50:55.790
in any institutions,
including ASEAN and UN.

00:50:55.790 --> 00:50:59.040
This is the very dangerous
for the longer run.

00:50:59.040 --> 00:51:02.203
- But I wanted to add to that,

00:51:03.117 --> 00:51:08.010
that actual intervention
by UN or foreign powers

00:51:08.010 --> 00:51:10.080
is off the table.

00:51:10.080 --> 00:51:12.970
I think we should start
with that assumption.

00:51:12.970 --> 00:51:14.653
However, there are lots of things

00:51:14.653 --> 00:51:18.800
that the international community can do.

00:51:18.800 --> 00:51:21.090
One of them is to put even more pressure

00:51:21.090 --> 00:51:26.060
on the nonpartisan delivery
of humanitarian aid

00:51:26.060 --> 00:51:27.650
in Burma.

00:51:27.650 --> 00:51:30.810
To communities that is not just

00:51:30.810 --> 00:51:32.680
distributed by the Tatmadaw.

00:51:32.680 --> 00:51:34.220
Another thing they can do

00:51:34.220 --> 00:51:37.320
is to persuade the Thai
military to give the people

00:51:37.320 --> 00:51:40.690
who were fleeing to Chiang Mai and Mae Sot

00:51:42.130 --> 00:51:44.990
temporary protected status...

00:51:45.880 --> 00:51:48.680
As political refugees.

00:51:48.680 --> 00:51:51.200
So that they are not jumping to arrests

00:51:51.200 --> 00:51:56.200
by the Thai military, and
being thrown back into Myanmar.

00:51:56.220 --> 00:52:00.957
And presumably that same thing
goes for the 11,500 people

00:52:00.957 --> 00:52:02.033
who were in Mizoram.

00:52:03.780 --> 00:52:07.420
And who fled to the the West,

00:52:07.420 --> 00:52:09.403
many of them Chin, as David knows.

00:52:10.690 --> 00:52:13.960
It seems to me that short of intervention,

00:52:13.960 --> 00:52:18.680
which will never happen,
a forcible intervention.

00:52:18.680 --> 00:52:20.267
There are a lot of things that

00:52:20.267 --> 00:52:21.887
the international community can do

00:52:21.887 --> 00:52:23.983
that have not yet been accomplished.

00:52:26.330 --> 00:52:30.140
- If I can add to the conversation,

00:52:30.140 --> 00:52:32.470
there are two questions
that international community

00:52:32.470 --> 00:52:34.540
can begin to ponder on.

00:52:34.540 --> 00:52:39.540
One is considering the
type and amount of violence

00:52:39.700 --> 00:52:42.560
that military is injecting on the public.

00:52:42.560 --> 00:52:45.620
And we are daily reading Facebook feed,

00:52:45.620 --> 00:52:47.250
Twitters and all that.

00:52:47.250 --> 00:52:49.350
The question for international community

00:52:49.350 --> 00:52:51.340
within the UN charter

00:52:51.340 --> 00:52:54.580
chapter one, six and seven to consider is,

00:52:54.580 --> 00:52:59.210
is the Myanmar military,
considering that it lost the trust.

00:52:59.210 --> 00:53:02.560
And the legitimacy of
all types within Myanmar,

00:53:02.560 --> 00:53:05.640
is Myanmar military becoming

00:53:05.640 --> 00:53:08.832
the world's largest
terrorist organization.

00:53:08.832 --> 00:53:12.060
Meaning a threat to not
just a domestic people,

00:53:12.060 --> 00:53:13.610
meaning citizens of Myanmar .

00:53:13.610 --> 00:53:15.970
But also the threat to the integrity

00:53:15.970 --> 00:53:17.810
of the United Nations charter,

00:53:17.810 --> 00:53:20.710
that enshrine the state sovereignty.

00:53:20.710 --> 00:53:24.770
And is Myanmar military delivering,

00:53:24.770 --> 00:53:26.700
insulting stakes over Myanmar,

00:53:26.700 --> 00:53:28.210
and giving to another party?

00:53:28.210 --> 00:53:29.660
Which would be to China,

00:53:29.660 --> 00:53:33.180
the Myanmar military
might be actually now.

00:53:33.180 --> 00:53:35.010
In fact, if you listen to the

00:53:35.010 --> 00:53:38.554
several interaction between
peace minister (indistinct),

00:53:38.554 --> 00:53:40.260
under the Taze administration

00:53:40.260 --> 00:53:42.090
with the (indistinct) villagers.

00:53:42.090 --> 00:53:44.600
Almen himself admitted
that Myanmar military

00:53:44.600 --> 00:53:46.180
could not override whatever China's

00:53:46.180 --> 00:53:47.550
want to do in the Myanmar.

00:53:47.550 --> 00:53:50.040
Meaning that they had to listen to China.

00:53:50.040 --> 00:53:52.900
So the question to that,
integrity of the UN charter,

00:53:52.900 --> 00:53:55.660
integrity of the state sovereign
of Myanmar is under threat.

00:53:55.660 --> 00:53:57.060
That question needs to be, I think,

00:53:57.060 --> 00:53:59.360
at the UN security council level consider.

00:53:59.360 --> 00:54:03.290
Second question is in
terms of the intervention,

00:54:03.290 --> 00:54:05.933
and although type of
intervention the Myanmar,

00:54:05.933 --> 00:54:08.310
the international
community might consider.

00:54:08.310 --> 00:54:11.960
I think we might really be
critically now forced to,

00:54:11.960 --> 00:54:13.147
in the case of Myanmar,

00:54:13.147 --> 00:54:15.960
or the case of the way
of violence in Myanmar

00:54:15.960 --> 00:54:16.899
by the military.

00:54:16.899 --> 00:54:21.161
Is there a type of a war that is just?

00:54:21.161 --> 00:54:24.360
In terms of guiding the
type of intervention

00:54:24.360 --> 00:54:26.670
that need to be done to protect

00:54:26.670 --> 00:54:31.300
the foundation of the
world, the community,

00:54:31.300 --> 00:54:33.650
which is the individual,
agency and freedom.

00:54:33.650 --> 00:54:37.323
If individual agency and
freedom become a dire threat,

00:54:38.690 --> 00:54:43.690
and if our freedom, if I'm
pointed a gun on my forehead,

00:54:44.210 --> 00:54:47.560
I should have a just
response to that person

00:54:47.560 --> 00:54:50.950
who is going to kill me, who
is going to threaten my life.

00:54:50.950 --> 00:54:52.030
So with that question,

00:54:52.030 --> 00:54:55.770
I think that the extrapolating
the just war theory.

00:54:55.770 --> 00:54:59.410
And understanding
September 7th defensive war

00:54:59.410 --> 00:55:03.437
that NUG prepare, not
declare, I would say.

00:55:03.437 --> 00:55:04.771
It's just preparation,

00:55:04.771 --> 00:55:06.670
they use in emergency situation,

00:55:06.670 --> 00:55:08.710
just for the people to prepare for it.

00:55:08.710 --> 00:55:12.080
I think that kind of
questions are becoming

00:55:12.080 --> 00:55:14.760
more relevant for the future diplomacy,

00:55:14.760 --> 00:55:17.300
and the iteration order.

00:55:17.300 --> 00:55:19.490
Because United State may not declare

00:55:19.490 --> 00:55:20.887
that we support the violence.

00:55:20.887 --> 00:55:24.246
But the United State have
invaded so many countries,

00:55:24.246 --> 00:55:26.420
and tried to change the
history of the country.

00:55:26.420 --> 00:55:27.370
If you think about it,

00:55:27.370 --> 00:55:30.750
if you think of these
within the just war theory

00:55:30.750 --> 00:55:33.640
that Obama eloquently
spoke at his Nobel Peace.

00:55:33.640 --> 00:55:36.140
These are kinds of questions
we need to really consider

00:55:36.140 --> 00:55:38.510
for international diplomats and scholars.

00:55:38.510 --> 00:55:40.510
If we don't what we are doing is

00:55:40.510 --> 00:55:44.470
we're basically listening or
watching that pendulum swinging

00:55:44.470 --> 00:55:47.080
between Nazi type of fascism regime

00:55:47.080 --> 00:55:49.110
to the democratic open type.

00:55:49.110 --> 00:55:52.400
And that will be the world
history for maybe five millennia,

00:55:52.400 --> 00:55:53.750
or eight millennia to come.

00:55:53.750 --> 00:55:56.800
And that is a shame if we
are learning from books,

00:55:56.800 --> 00:55:58.670
and thinking about these problems,

00:55:58.670 --> 00:56:01.300
and the nature of this
violence that we cannot accept.

00:56:01.300 --> 00:56:02.910
Even to see on the screen,

00:56:02.910 --> 00:56:06.960
that is what at stake
for a lot of education,

00:56:06.960 --> 00:56:09.410
and institution, and practitioners.

00:56:09.410 --> 00:56:10.813
That's where I would add to.

